"""The Props Book: things to make with paper, glue, air-dry clay and black yarn. Used by build_props.py."""

BLOCKS = [
    ('chapter', 'Props', 'Things to hold in your hands', 'Paper, glue, air-dry clay and black yarn. Every prop here matches something that already happens in the game, so the phone and the prop always agree.'),
    ('p', 'Your players loved the props on the first night, so this book gives you more of them. Each one turns up at a moment the game already has: a clue they pick up, a quest reward, a trial on Day 7. '
          'When the app gives them the item, you hand them the real thing. The templates at the back are printed at the right size: print them at <b>100%</b> (not “fit to page”), cut inside the lines, and glue.'),
    ('h2', 'Before you start'),
    ('list', [
        '<b>Air-dry clay needs time.</b> Small pieces dry in a day, thick ones take two or three. Make everything a few days before the session, and turn pieces over halfway so the bottom dries too.',
        '<b>Keep clay at least as thick as a pencil.</b> Thin edges crack and snap when they dry. If a crack appears, smooth in a little wet clay.',
        '<b>Press, don’t carve.</b> Runes and pictures look best pressed into soft clay with a toothpick, the tip of a pencil, a fork or a paper clip.',
        '<b>Make paper look old.</b> Brush on strong cold tea or coffee, let it dry, then crumple it and flatten it again. Tear the edges instead of cutting them. Never burn them.',
        '<b>Colour without paint.</b> Rub pencil graphite or a black marker into pressed lines and wipe the top clean, so the lines stay dark. Yellow or orange marker makes clay look gold.',
        '<b>Black yarn ties everything.</b> Roll scrolls and tie them, hang tags from it, and keep a long tangle for the Devil’s Snare.',
    ]),
    ('h2', 'What to make, and when it comes out'),
    ('table', ['Prop', 'Made from', 'Comes out when', 'Template'], [
        ['Secret gift cards', 'Paper, clay seal, yarn', 'Start of session 2, with the student books', 'T1'],
        ['Family keepsakes', 'Paper, clay tag, yarn', 'Session 2 (or when the secret comes up)', 'T2'],
        ['The Golden Snitch', 'Clay, paper wings', 'The Old Cup quest (after detention), at the Astronomy Tower', 'T3'],
        ['Rune tablets (3)', 'Clay', 'Day 1 Library, Day 2 Greenhouses, Day 3 Hagrid’s hut', 'T4'],
        ['Ring clues (3)', 'Paper', 'Day 1: Trophy Room, Astronomy Tower (inside the Snitch), Owlery', 'T5'],
        ['The Turning Lock', 'Paper discs and a pencil', 'Days 5 to 6: the Room of Requirement', 'T6'],
        ['Vale’s diary (3 pages)', 'Paper', 'Day 3 Restricted Section, Day 4 Black Lake, Day 6 Shrieking Shack', 'T7'],
        ['The torn map edge', 'Paper', 'Fenwick’s half now. The other half on Day 4', 'T7'],
        ['Owl letter seals', 'Clay, yarn', 'Whenever you send an owl with a parcel', 'none'],
        ['Devil’s Snare', 'Black yarn', 'Day 7: the first of the Trials Beneath', 'none'],
        ['The Seven Bottles', 'Clay, paper labels', 'Day 7: the last trial', 'T8'],
        ['The Mirror Maze (4 house maps)', 'Ordinary printer paper', 'Day 1 Free time: the tall mirror in the Library', 'T9, T10'],
        ['Relic cards', 'Paper or card', 'When a relic is earned', 'T11'],
    ], [42, 34, 70, 18]),

    ('h2', 'Is there Quidditch?'),
    ('p', 'Not a match, no. But Quidditch is in the castle: Madam Hooch teaches <b>Flying Lessons</b> on the Quidditch Pitch, the Trophy Room is full of old cups, '
          'and one hidden quest, <b>The Old Cup</b>, starts with a Quidditch trophy. That is where your Golden Snitch goes.'),

    ('h2', 'The Golden Snitch'),
    ('p', '<b>The story.</b> Long ago, a forgotten house team won the cup in the Trophy Room. Their Seeker kept the winning Snitch and hid something inside it at the top of the Astronomy Tower. '
          'When a student polishes that cup in detention, they read the engraving: “Where the stars are closest, the second ring waits.” At the Astronomy Tower they find the Snitch, and inside it, the star chart for the Turning Lock.'),
    ('h3', 'How to make it'),
    ('list', [
        'Roll a ball of clay the size of a walnut. Push a pencil about two thirds of the way in, wiggle it to make a hole, and pull it out.',
        'Press two curved lines around the middle with a toothpick, like seams. With a knife, cut a thin slit on each side for the wings.',
        'Let it dry for two days. Colour it gold with a yellow or orange marker (or wrap it in yellow paper and glue it down).',
        'Cut the two wings from template T3, fold each along its spine for strength, and glue them into the slits.',
        'Roll the star chart (T5) tightly, tie it with a short piece of black yarn, and slide it into the hole.',
    ]),
    ('h3', 'At the table'),
    ('p', 'The moment they reach the Astronomy Tower on The Old Cup, put the Snitch on the table in front of them, before the app says anything. Let them work out that something is inside. '
          'Then complete the objective on the GM screen, and the star chart appears in their Bag.'),
    ('tip', 'Make a second, smaller Snitch with no scroll. On the day of Flying Lessons, hide it somewhere in the real room before the session. The first student who spots it and catches it earns <b>+5 house points</b>. Madam Hooch is impressed.'),

    ('h2', 'The rune tablets'),
    ('p', 'Three slates, each with three pictures and the word for each picture carved underneath in runes. They are how the players learn the rune alphabet. The app already shows them on the phone; the clay version is what they pass around the table.'),
    ('h3', 'How to make them'),
    ('list', [
        'Roll clay flat, about as thick as your little finger, and cut a slab the size of a phone (or a little smaller). Break a corner off and stick it back on with a smear of wet clay, so it looks cracked and mended.',
        'Lay template T4 on top and press through the paper along each rune with a toothpick, then lift the paper and deepen the lines. Draw each little picture the same way.',
        'Let them dry, then rub graphite or black marker into the lines and wipe the surface. The <b>hearth</b> slate is soot-black: rub graphite over the whole thing. The <b>garden</b> slate is mossy: a little green marker in the cracks.',
    ]),
    ('tip', 'Hand over the slate when they pick the tablet up in the app. They keep it until the end of the campaign, next to their rune notebook. Watching them hold two slates side by side to compare runes is the best part.'),

    ('h2', 'The ring clues and the Turning Lock'),
    ('p', 'Three clues, each naming one symbol: a scratched note (Trophy Room), a star chart (Astronomy Tower, inside the Snitch) and a feathered card (Owlery). Together they open the Turning Lock in the Room of Requirement.'),
    ('list', [
        '<b>Clues (T5):</b> print, tea-stain and cut out. Scratch over the note with a pin so it looks clawed. Fold the feathered card in half and glue a paper feather to the front.',
        '<b>The lock (T6):</b> glue the base and the three rings onto cereal-box card before cutting them out. Stack them, biggest at the bottom, and push a pencil through all four centres. The arrow on the base stays still; the rings turn. (The base is only a little bigger than ring 1, so the arrow peeks out above it.)',
        '<b>Clay version:</b> make three thick clay rings that slide onto the pencil, and press the six symbols into the edge of each, in the order printed on T6.',
    ]),
    ('tip', 'Put the paper lock in front of them the moment they find the chest. Let them turn it with their hands while the puzzle is up on the TV. When they think they have it, they say it out loud and you solve it on the GM screen.'),

    ('h2', 'Vale’s diary and the torn map'),
    ('p', '<b>The diary (T7)</b> is three pages in Vale’s runes. Tea-stain them dark, tear the edges, and give each page as it is found. Once they have all three, they lay them side by side and decode the whole thing.'),
    ('p', '<b>The map</b> is one sheet torn in two. Draw a rough castle on an old-looking sheet of paper (copy the TV map if you like), then tear it down the middle with a zig-zag tear. '
          'Glue the rune strip from T7 along the torn edge of the left half. That half belongs to the Fenwick from the start: give it to them at the next session. Keep the right half yourself. '
          'On Day 4, when they win it from Cassius Thorne, hand it over and let them fit the torn edges together.'),

    ('h2', 'Owl letter seals'),
    ('p', 'When you send someone a parcel by owl, give them a real folded letter as well. Press a ball of clay flat onto a circle of paper, and stamp it with the end of a pen, a button, a coin edge or a fork before it dries. '
          'Make a handful at once and keep them in a box. Fold the letter, tie it with black yarn, and push a dry seal under the knot with a dot of glue.'),
    ('tip', 'A sleeping draught for the student with the snoring dorm-mate: a tiny clay bottle on a piece of yarn, tied to the letter.'),

    ('h2', 'The Devil’s Snare'),
    ('p', 'Before Day 7, cut five or six long pieces of black yarn and tangle them loosely together. When the party falls into the Devil’s Snare, drop the tangle over their hands on the table.'),
    ('read', 'The vines curl around your wrists. The more you struggle, the tighter they hold.'),
    ('p', 'Each time a player says they struggle, pull the yarn a little tighter. It lets go when someone casts light or fire at it (Lumos Solem or Incendio), or when they all go still and calm. Then the fight on the GM screen begins (or ends).'),

    ('h2', 'The Seven Bottles'),
    ('p', 'Seven little clay bottles: two tall, two small, two round and one tiny. Let them dry, then wrap each in its paper label from T8 (number and colour). Line them up in order, and put the riddle card in front of them.'),
    ('tip', 'Let them pick up the bottles and move them around while they argue. When they agree, the one who drinks lifts the bottle and says its number, and you solve the puzzle on the GM screen.'),

    ('h2', 'Secret gifts and family keepsakes'),
    ('p', 'At the start of session 2, when you hand back the student books, slip each player a sealed card (T1). Inside is the secret they told you about, written as a gift: what it does every day, what it does once a day, and its drawback. '
          'Give the same gift on the GM screen too (Players tab, <b>Give a secret gift</b>), so it is on their phone.'),
    ('p', '<b>Keepsakes (T2)</b> are one small paper object for each family’s secret. Each is a reminder of the mystery they arrived with. Give them out with the gift cards, or the first time that secret matters.'),
    ('table', ['Family', 'Gift', 'A clay token to go with it (optional)'], [
        ['Blackwood', 'Spider-Speech', 'Spiders were left out of the game on purpose before, so keep them to words: no clay spiders and no spider pictures, unless you have checked with your table first.'],
        ['Sinclair', 'Fashionably Late', 'A clay pocket watch: a flat disc with a loop of yarn, its hands pressed in at a quarter past.'],
        ['Fenwick', 'Green Thumb', 'A little clay seed pod, or a Mandrake root with a tiny angry face.'],
        ['Thornbury', 'Rat-Speech', 'A tiny clay rat with a black yarn tail.'],
        ['Quill', 'Mirror Self', 'A small round clay hand-mirror, with a circle of foil or shiny paper glued in.'],
        ['Penrose', 'The Battered Suitcase', 'A matchbox-sized paper suitcase with the letters N.S. on the handle, and the three creature cards from T1 inside.'],
        ['Okafor', 'The Invisibility Cloak', 'A small square of grey paper folded like a cloak, with “Do not lose” written inside.'],
    ], [24, 38, 108]),

    ('chapter', 'The Mirror in the Dark', 'Four maps, one way out', 'On Day 1, a tall mirror in the Library drops the whole party into a pitch-black maze. Each house holds one old map. Alone, every map leads to a dead end. Together, held up to the light, they show the only way out, and something watching.'),
    ('h2', 'How it works'),
    ('list', [
        '<b>Four maps (T9), one per house.</b> Long ago, four students escaped this mirror: one from each house. Each drew the part of the maze they remembered, and signed it. Give each house its own map. Nobody is told they are the same maze.',
        '<b>Each map is right from its YOU star to the centre</b>, so every house can find the middle.',
        '<b>Beyond the centre, every map lies a little.</b> It shows only some of the walls, and its own EXIT, which is really bricked up by walls drawn on the other three maps.',
        '<b>Stacked and held against the lit TV</b>, the walls add up to the real maze and every EXIT closes. Each map also carries one quarter of some little circles: only where all four agree does a circle come out whole, and the whole circles mark the way out.',
        '<b>The secret in the stack:</b> each map also hides one quarter of a picture. Alone it is a few odd curves. Stacked, a great painted eye appears round the centre of the maze, with the words <b>HE SEES YOU</b> beneath it. The four signatures line up neatly at the top, one under another.',
        '<b>T10 is your key.</b> The GM screen shows the same maze while the puzzle is up, with everyone moving on it, and the watcher.',
    ]),
    ('h2', 'Making the maps'),
    ('list', [
        '<b>Print T9 at 100%</b> (actual size). Ordinary printer paper is fine: four sheets held flat against a bright TV show through clearly.',
        '<b>Or draw them by hand:</b> <i>Mirror-Maze-Tracing.pdf</i> has a tracing master for each house, in its mapmaker’s own handwriting, with their notes and a doodle in the margins. Print the masters, tape one to a bright window under aged paper, and trace in pen. Its first page explains how.',
        '<b>Test it before the night:</b> stack all four, line up the four crosshairs in the corners, and hold them against a white screen (or a window). You should see the whole circles, the eye and the words.',
        'Age them a little if you like (a light wash of cold tea, dried flat), but keep it light so the stack still shines through. Fold each map in four.',
    ]),
    ('h2', 'Running it (Day 1, Free time)'),
    ('read', 'At the far end of the Library, past the last shelf, stands a mirror taller than a door. As you pass, its glass ripples like water. Before anyone can step back, it swallows you whole. Then: nothing. Pitch black, and cold stone underfoot. There is a folded piece of parchment in your hand. And somewhere in the dark, something is breathing.'),
    ('gm', 'Puzzles tab, <b>The Mirror in the Dark</b>, <b>Put it on the TV</b>. The TV and every phone go black, and the dark hums and drips. Hand each house its map. Everyone taps <b>Open your eyes</b>.'),
    ('list', [
        '<b>Walking:</b> each phone starts on its house’s YOU, with four arrows. It knows the walls: walking into one says <i>Thud</i>. The phone never shows the maze, so they follow the paper with a finger. Housemates share one map and have to talk.',
        '<b>The watcher:</b> a cold presence drifts through the dark, and hunts anyone it gets near. When it is close their phone thumps like a heartbeat and they feel breath on their neck. If it reaches them, they are thrown back to their start (1d4). It can’t enter the centre: that is the safe room.',
        '<b>Past the centre, one bump throws them back to their start</b> (1d4). A lone map always ends at a bricked-up EXIT.',
        '<b>The pedestal:</b> when someone from every house stands in the centre, a stone pedestal lights on the TV and on their phones, with a riddle: <i>“Four roads end here, and so does the light. The more of me there is, the less you see. This morning you learned the word that ends me.”</i> (Darkness: they learned Lumos in Charms that morning.)',
        '<b>Lumos:</b> the phones at the pedestal are quietly listening. Nothing tells the players so. The moment anyone says <i>Lumos</i> out loud, the TV and every phone flood with white light. If they only whisper it, or a phone doesn’t hear, press <b>They said it</b> on the GM screen.',
        '<b>The answer:</b> they hold their four maps, stacked and lined up, flat against the bright TV. The whole circles lead to the way out, and the eye stares back at them. Then each of them walks it.',
        '<b>When everyone is out</b>, the puzzle solves itself and the phones come back.',
    ]),
    ('tip', 'Let the eye land. When the stack goes up against the TV and someone spots it, stop talking. Then say, very quietly: “Who drew that?” Nobody did. It was in every map all along.'),
    ('tip', 'Some Android phones can turn on their real torch from the page (a <b>Brighter</b> button appears once the light is lit). iPhones can’t, so players just turn their screen brightness up. The first time the pedestal listens, the phone asks for the microphone: that is part of the magic. Say nothing.'),

    ('chapter', 'Relics', 'Legendary and mythic things', 'One of each in the whole world. Relics are rewards for big moments: a brave choice, a solved mystery, a kindness nobody asked for.'),
    ('p', 'Give a relic from the Players tab: <b>Give a relic</b>, pick it, and change any words first. It appears on that student’s phone as a card with its story, what it always does, what it does when used, and its drawback. Hand over the relic card (T11) and the prop at the same moment, in the story.'),
    ('table', ['Relic', 'Where it could turn up', 'Make it from'], [
        ['The Sword of Gryffindor (Mythic)', 'Day 7 in the Undercroft, pulled from the Sorting Hat after a truly brave act', 'A card blade covered in grey pencil, a clay hilt with red marker “rubies”, glued together. Hide it inside a hat.'],
        ['A Phoenix Tear (Mythic)', 'The Hospital Wing, after someone risks themselves for another', 'A tiny clay vial with a single drop of clear glue dried inside the neck.'],
        ['Vale’s First Brush (Mythic)', 'The Undercroft, or offered by the painting', 'A real paintbrush with its handle wrapped in black yarn, the bristles dipped in red marker.'],
        ['The Blackwood Seal Ring', 'A Blackwood portrait, or behind the oldest shield in the Trophy Room', 'A clay ring with a flat face. Press a B into it while soft: it then really stamps your clay seals.'],
        ['The Lantern of the Dark Room', 'The way out of the Mirror in the Dark', 'A paper lantern: a tube of paper with windows cut out, a clay base and a yarn handle. Put a phone torch inside.'],
        ['The Moonlit Compass', 'The Astronomy Tower at night, or the ancient chest', 'A clay disc with a paper needle on a pin, so it really spins. No letters on it.'],
        ['The Old Boot', 'Under the floorboards of the Shrieking Shack', 'A real old shoe. Everyone grabs it on three.'],
        ['The Twin Cabinet Key', 'The Room of Requirement', 'A big clay key with a cabinet shape pressed into the bow.'],
        ['The Goblin-Silver Dagger', 'The cursed chest, or by owl from the Okafors', 'A card blade covered in pencil graphite (it shines silver), with a clay handle.'],
        ['The Silver Memory', 'The Restricted Section, or from the Headmaster', 'A clay vial with a twist of shiny paper inside. When it is poured, read the memory aloud.'],
    ], [44, 56, 70]),
    ('h3', 'A memory for the Silver Memory'),
    ('read', 'Candlelight in the Great Hall, three hundred years ago. A tall man in a paint-flecked coat stands at an easel while a girl in Hufflepuff yellow sits very still for him. “Don’t move,” he says kindly. “I only need a little more of your colour.” She smiles. With every stroke, she grows a little greyer, and she doesn’t notice. Behind him, on the wall, a dozen finished portraits are watching, and every one of them is a student.'),
    ('tip', 'Relics are strong on purpose, and each has a cost. Use the drawbacks: a Gringotts letter arriving at breakfast, the painting whispering to the ring-bearer at night, the boot landing everyone in the lake. A relic should make the story bigger, not shorter.'),
]
